Implemented and locally verified the native Track B generator.

The paper’s Theorem 2 is existential, while Section IV leaves explicit construction as future work, so a Track A claim would be unsupported. The module instead uses symplectic transformations with a carried radical certificate, as grounded in Section II of the [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.00668).

Local results:

- G1–G8 pass.
- Reference algorithm: 12,150,796 field operations.
- Compact route: 291 field operations.
- Guessing: 0/200,000.
- Six attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- Answer: 226 characters, 59 elements.
- G8: 140/140 symmetry checks.

Files:

- [gen_2003_00668.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2003.00668/gen_2003_00668.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2003.00668/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2003.00668/README.md)
- [bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2003.00668/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2003.00668/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2003.00668/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The remaining required gate is blocked: all three hardening runs received OpenRouter HTTP 403, “Key limit exceeded (total limit).” These errors cannot count as oracle failures, so G9 and `all_passed` remain false intentionally.

Please replenish or replace `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, then ask me to continue; I can rerun the three oracle arms and finalize the shipping verdict.
